# Build Mode

Replace offhand build-hammer activation with a rebindable build-mode toggle,
reusing the existing brush, placement, preview, and ordered state synchronization.

## Phase 1: Settle behavior
    ☑ Choose the default toggle key and enabled/disabled feedback.
    ☑ Confirm lifecycle behavior, including hotbar changes, unsupported items, snapping preferences, and death/respawn.
    ☑ Decide how existing saved hammers are handled.

Approved behavior:
    - Toggle: Ctrl+B, rebindable through game controls; localized "Build mode enabled" / "Build mode disabled" notices. No persistent HUD indicator in this change. Check the default against game bindings before implementation.
    - Lifecycle: off on join and death/respawn; retain mode across hotbar changes, suspend brush use for unsupported items, and retain snapping preferences across toggles within the session.
    - Suspended brush use: keep build mode enabled, hide the preview, and preserve ordinary tool/item interactions while the selected hotbar slot is empty or fails existing brush block eligibility rules. Resume brush use automatically when a supported block is selected. Lack of rotation support does not make a block unsupported.
    - Existing hammers: retain a hidden legacy item with no build-mode activation; remove crafting and creative availability.

## Phase 2: Implement mode activation and synchronization
    ☐ Add the localized, rebindable toggle and explicit enter/exit handling; refresh the source item and selection on entry and clear the preview on exit.
    ☐ Replace client/server offhand-driven activation and the client hammer placement check with mode state; rename equipment lifecycle methods accordingly.
    ☐ Send every mode transition immediately through the existing sequenced brush packet, including when there is no target; ensure activation/deactivation and immediate placement are applied consistently by both sides.
    ☐ Replace hammer activation tests with toggle/lifecycle coverage; cover empty selection, unsupported items, hotbar changes, and repeated toggles.
    ☐ Test ordered mode updates, stale packets, and immediate placement around entry/exit; verify preview cleanup and return to normal placement.

## Phase 3: Integrate input and retire the hammer
    ☐ Gate rotation, snapping, face-offset, and placement input appropriately; preserve normal interactions when brush use is suspended or mode is off, and clear transient held-key state on exit.
    ☐ Remove hammer crafting/creative availability and equipment helpers; handle item registration/assets according to the saved-item decision and update affected names and user documentation.

## Phase 4: Validate the completed change
    ☐ Have a subagent run the relevant build and tests; resolve failures and review the final diff against this tasklist.
    ☐ User-run in-game checks: key rebinding, mode feedback, offhand items, tool interactions, multiplayer toggles, reconnect/respawn, and existing-save hammer handling.
